se a CAS to perform the following steps:"},{"x":0.08775,"y":0.35171,"w":0.35127,"h":0.01327,"fs":0.01151,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"a. Plot the function near the point c being approached."},{"x":0.08775,"y":0.36962,"w":0.30224,"h":0.01327,"fs":0.01151,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"b. From your plot guess the value of the limit."},{"x":0.08775,"y":0.42624,"w":0.37202,"h":0.04229,"fs":0.03581,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"2.3 The Precise Definition of a Limit"},{"x":0.36258,"y":0.47495,"w":0.59307,"h":0.07566,"fs":0.01279,"n":5,"k":1,"t":"We now turn our attention to the precise definition of a limit. We replace vague phrases like “gets arbitrarily close to” in the informal definition with specific conditions that can be applied to any particular example. With a precise definition, we can avoid misunder- standings, prove the limit properties given in the preceding section, and establish many important limits."},{"x":0.36258,"y":0.5501,"w":0.59308,"h":0.0619,"fs":0.01279,"n":4,"k":1,"t":"To show that the limit of ƒ(x) as x S c equals the number L, we need to show that the gap between ƒ(x) and L can be made “as small as we choose” if x is kept “close enough” to c. Let us see what this would require if we specified the size of the gap between ƒ(x) and L."},{"x":0.36258,"y":0.64543,"w":0.59304,"h":0.03412,"fs":0.01407,"n":2,"k":0,"t":"Example 1 Consider the function y = 2x - 1 near x = 4. Intuitively it appears that y is close to 7 when x is close to 4, so limxS4 (2x - 1) = 7. However, how close to"},{"x":0.36382,"y":0.67767,"w":0.55302,"h":0.01723,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"x = 4 does x have to be so that y = 2x - 1 differs from 7 by, say, less than 2 units?"},{"x":0.36259,"y":0.68037,"w":0.59302,"h":0.09269,"fs":0.01407,"n":2,"k":0,"t":"Solution We are asked: For what values of x is 0 y - 70 6 2? To find the answer we first express 0 y - 70 in terms of x:"},{"x":0.19501,"y":0.68254,"w":0.06058,"h":0.01164,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"y = 2x − 1"},{"x":0.25589,"y":0.71179,"w":0.07233,"h":0.02392,"fs":0.01023,"n":2,"k":0,"t":"Upper bound: y = 9"},{"x":0.54075,"y":0.71874,"w":0.26451,"h":0.0776,"fs":0.01353,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"0 y - 70 = 0 (2x - 1) - 70 = 0 2x - 80 ."},{"x":0.08879,"y":0.7339,"w":0.07201,"h":0.03593,"fs":0.01023,"n":3,"k":1,"t":"9 To satisfy this"},{"x":0.36258,"y":0.74176,"w":0.59304,"h":0.07735,"fs":0.01353,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"The question then becomes: what values of x satisfy the inequality 0 2x - 80 6 2? To find out, we solve the inequality:"},{"x":0.25589,"y":0.78253,"w":0.07379,"h":0.02392,"fs":0.01023,"n":2,"k":0,"t":"Lower bound: y = 5"},{"x":0.54581,"y":0.78396,"w":0.16423,"h":0.07735,"fs":0.01353,"n":2,"k":0,"t":"0 2x - 80 6 2 -2 6 2x - 8 6 2"},{"x":0.20087,"y":0.83366,"w":0.03246,"h":0.01142,"fs":0.01023,"n":3,"k":0,"t":"3 5 4"},{"x":0.19668,"y":0.85274,"w":0.04071,"h":0.0237,"fs":0.01023,"n":2,"k":0,"t":"Restrict to this"},{"x":0.58327,"y":0.85352,"w":0.24747,"h":0.05559,"fs":0.01279,"n":5,"k":0,"t":"6 6 2x 6 10 3 6 x 6 5 Solve for x. -1 6 x - 4 6 1. Solve for x - 4."},{"x":0.08775,"y":0.88617,"w":0.24577,"h":0.0312,"fs":0.01151,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"Figure 2.15 Keeping x within 1 unit of x = 4 will keep y within 2 units of"},{"x":0.08899,"y":0.91721,"w":0.11713,"h":0.0155,"fs":0.01151,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"y = 7 (Example 1)."},{"x":0.36258,"y":0.91939,"w":0.54979,"h":0.01723,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"Keeping x within 1 unit of x = 4 will keep y within 2 units of y = 7 (Figure 2.15)."}]}